helping hands of henderson they were  founded back in march of 1995 this year  now marks the 25 years in which they  have served our community  when the pandemic hit like many of the  programs had so many challenges to face  a news now reporter sally jeremiah  shares their struggles and their  victories  our helping hands of henderson program  typically serves about 450  seniors in the city of henderson helping  hands is known for providing the elderly  with transportation to medical  appointments grocery shopping  social activities exercise and wellness  programs  when covid hit a lot of those senior  services closed up  during that time we had to shift pretty  quickly to figure out how can we serve  the needs that they were facing now  dignity health community manager  jennifer finlay says for most other  seniors  that meant isolation and food access  currently we're  serving anywhere from 150 to 200 seniors  a month  with home delivered groceries they've  teamed up with a number of community  agencies  despite the partners there were  limitations  we typically had a staff of 10 and a  volunteer crew of 50  that would provide transportation as  well as  delivering food and calling clients if  we needed to  so we weren't able to use our volunteers  anymore i think that every one of us  felt like overwhelmed at times  but none of us want to quit with just 10  staff  they are working to help seniors through  their deliberate human  kindness kits as well as providing  reassurance calls just to say hi  we're still here how are you doing what  do you need  cerise mcallister is a helping hands  clerk she says the best part of the  program  is the bonds they create with seniors  like her client bob  whom she takes to the heritage senior  aquatic center he just called this  morning and told us how much he  appreciates us  and what he wouldn't know what to do  without us their message to their  seniors  that we love them and we miss them a lot  sally garamillo 8 news now  thank you sally we are stronger together  join us tomorrow evening  at 8 30 p.m as we look back at how our  community has come together during the  cobit pandemic  again saturday night at 8 30 p.m we'll  be right back
 

